<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1417">1417</a>–<a href="/years/1468">1468</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-family">Malatesta family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta was lord of Rimini from 1432 and one of the leading condottieri of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tempio-malatestiano">Tempio Malatestiano</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-battista-alberti">Leon Battista Alberti</a> and the Castel Sismondo. His quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> led to his excommunication and the loss of most of his lands in 1463.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismondo-pandolfo-malatesta.jpg" alt="Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Piero della Francesca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piero,_ritratto_di_sigismondo_malatesta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> on 19 June 1417, the elder of the two illegitimate sons of Pandolfo III Malatesta. Following family tradition, he first served as a man-at-arms at the age of 13 against his relative Carlo II Malatesta of Pesaro, who sought to annex Rimini, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesena">Cesena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fano">Fano</a>. After his victory he and his brothers obtained the title of papal vicar for those cities. When his elder brother abdicated, he became lord of Rimini at the age of 15 and of Fano from 1432.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sigismondo commanded a papal corps in 1432 but was excommunicated after occupying the papal city of Cervia, and was soon pardoned and made commander of the papal army. He fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, whom he later betrayed, and served in turn under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, Siena and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. In 1445 he forced the Neapolitans to raise the siege of Piombino. Two of his wives died in mysterious circumstances, and rumours of poisoning and drowning spread, though the second charge, made by Sforza, was never confirmed. In 1456 he married his long-time mistress Isotta degli Atti.</p><p>The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> in 1454, from which he was excluded, turned the major Italian powers against him. On 25 December 1460 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> tried him in his absence, excommunicated him as a heretic and accused him of a series of sins. His image was burnt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and a league of the pope, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-da-montefeltro">Federico da Montefeltro</a> fought him. After defeats near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sena-gallica">Senigallia</a> in 1462, the war ended in 1463 with the loss of all his territories except Rimini and about 8 kilometres around it. He then served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> as a field commander in the Peloponnesus from 1464 to 1466.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sigismondo died at Castel Sismondo in 1468 and was succeeded by his legitimated son Sallustio. His contemporaries recognised his skill as a general, and he built the Rocche of Rimini and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fano">Fano</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tempio-malatestiano">Tempio Malatestiano</a> in Rimini, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-battista-alberti">Leon Battista Alberti</a> and decorated by artists including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-della-francesca">Piero della Francesca</a> and Agostino di Duccio, was essentially a lay monument to Isotta and the first church to use the Roman triumphal arch as part of its structure. His reputation rested largely on Pius II's view of him, and chroniclers called him a tyrant. Ezra Pound's Malatesta Cantos admire him, and Adrian Stokes wrote The Stones of Rimini on the art of his court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta</b>, a brilliant and ruthless captain, had Leon Battista Alberti remodel the church of San Francesco as the Tempio Malatestiano.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-rimini">Lordship of Rimini</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Rimini, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.54,10.22&amp;year=1450" aria-label="Brescia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5609 6176h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5609 6176h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.54,10.22&amp;year=1450">What was here in 1450? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
